<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hisham II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">966–c. 1013</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Hisham II al-Muayyad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hisham II was the third Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, succeeding as a child in 976. Real power lay with the hajib <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a> and his sons, while Hisham was kept secluded. He was deposed in 1009, restored in 1010 and disappeared when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> troops took Córdoba in 1013. Impostors claiming to be him later lent legitimacy to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbadid-dynasty">Abbadids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hisham-ii.jpg" alt="Hisham II" width="320" height="308" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silver_dirham_LACMA_M.2002.1.437_(2_of_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
